  "verbalization": "Phylloquinone, through its indication for Nephrocalcinosis, appears to be linked to Phosphorus Metabolism Disease via a series of intermediate compounds—Calcium Acetate, Calcium Glucoheptonate, and Calcium Glycerophosphate. These compounds are all indicated to contribute to the development of Phosphorus Metabolism Disease, suggesting a potential pathway where Phylloquinone influences calcium metabolism, which in turn affects phosphorus homeostasis. The consistent scores across the three paths indicate a robust, albeit indirect, relationship between Phylloquinone and the disease, likely mediated through its role in calcium metabolism. The presence of multiple calcium salts in the indication chain suggests that Phylloquinone may modulate calcium ion levels, which could disrupt phosphorus metabolism, leading to the disease. While the exact mechanism remains unclear, the integrated evidence points to a possible dependency on calcium regulation as a key pathway.",
